#Differencing to Remove Trends: Differencing is a technique used to make a non-stationary 
#time series stationary by calculating the difference between consecutive observations. 
#this effectively removes the trend component

#How Differencing Works:
#First-order differencing: y'[t] = y[t] - y[t-1], where y[t] is the value at time t. This is usually sufficient to remove linear trends.
#Second-order differencing: If the first-order differencing doesn't remove the trend (e.g., if the trend is quadratic), 
#you can apply differencing again: y''[t] = y'[t] - y'[t-1].
#differenced data no longer has the upward trend of the original index.

